WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
(bell dings)

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.600
When programming, it is often necessary

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.920
to establish whether something is true

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:14.583
or false so that a computer can make decisions about what actions are appropriate to take

00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:15.903
next.

00:00:15.903 --> 00:00:19.967
For example, when deciding whether or not to provide voter registration information,

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:24.967
the computer must first determine if it is true that the person is old enough to vote.

00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:29.951
This is something that is done using what programmers call a Boolean expression.

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:34.951
You can think of it like a yes or no question that the computer can evaluate.

00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:39.935
It's called a Boolean because it's named after the mathematician

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:46.935
George Boole, who made many discoveries related to working with binary true or false values.

00:00:46.935 --> 00:00:56.679
In the voter registration example, your Boolean expression might look like this. This asks, is the value stored in the variable age equal

00:00:56.679 --> 00:00:59.903
to 18? If it is, then the statement

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:03.423
is true, otherwise the statement is false.

00:01:03.423 --> 00:01:07.623
Remember, the double equals is the equality operator.

00:01:07.623 --> 00:01:09.887
It asks if two things are equal

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:15.227
or not. If you are using a single equal sign, you are using the assignment operator to

00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:18.607
set the value of a variable, which is something else entirely.

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:26.871
In addition to the equality operator, we can ask questions about if one value is greater than or less than another value.

00:01:26.871 --> 00:01:29.855
And these are called comparison operators.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:34.655
If you saw the statement "3 less than 2" in math class, you'd think something was terribly

00:01:34.655 --> 00:01:35.655
wrong.

00:01:35.655 --> 00:01:38.415
But this is still not math class.

00:01:38.415 --> 00:01:39.815
In programming, you use the

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:44.839
comparison operators to ask the computer a question which it can answer true or

00:01:44.839 --> 00:01:49.319
false. So with this line, the computer sees you asking:

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:51.863
Is 3 less than 2?

00:01:51.863 --> 00:01:55.643
The answer in this case is no or false.

00:01:55.643 --> 00:01:59.623
We can use a comparison operator in our voter registration example to make the

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:01.807
program even more accurate.

00:02:01.807 --> 00:02:03.807
Because it's not just 18-year-olds who can vote,

00:02:03.807 --> 00:02:06.807
it's anyone whose age is 18 or older.

00:02:06.807 --> 00:02:09.791
So the question you'd want the computer to evaluate

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:15.791
should be: Is the user's age greater than or equal to 18? Like this.

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:25.495
Now that we can express the question, we can have the computer actually do something depending

00:02:25.495 --> 00:02:29.735
on whether the answer is true or false, such as displaying the registration

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:33.519
info if true. We'll learn more about how to do that next.
